  <p>Setup, escalation, consequence. Three shots. The first needs to be ordinary enough to give the others somewhere to go. I recognize that from editing: you feel the sequence working, and explaining each shot would only get in its way.</p>

  <p>Some attempts still fall flat. Jerry Seinfeld said on <a href="https://tim.blog/2020/12/09/jerry-seinfeld-transcript/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>The Tim Ferriss Show</em></a>, "Every opinion the audience gives you is 100 percent accurate." He was talking about the feedback a performer gets from a room. I take the point. If they didn't laugh, explaining why they should have probably isn't going to improve the evening.</p>

  <p>So I keep trying. I don't need a stage. A coworker hiding a smile or one of my kids groaning is plenty. The kid who wanted to be Mork is apparently still in there, trying to become the class clown forty years too late.</p>

  <p>Piglet, of course, hasn't had to practice any of this. She just went to sleep.</p>
